      "title": "File:Malik Ambar's Tomb, Khuldabad, Aurangabad district, Maharashtra 02.jpg",
      "source_page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malik_Ambar%27s_Tomb,_Khuldabad,_Aurangabad_district,_Maharashtra_02.jpg",
      "original_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Malik_Ambar%27s_Tomb%2C_Khuldabad%2C_Aurangabad_district%2C_Maharashtra_02.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "preview_url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Malik_Ambar%27s_Tomb%2C_Khuldabad%2C_Aurangabad_district%2C_Maharashtra_02.jpg/1280px-Malik_Ambar%27s_Tomb%2C_Khuldabad%2C_Aurangabad_district%2C_Maharashtra_02.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail",
      "original_width": 5888,
      "original_height": 3920,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0",
      "author": "Amitabha Gupta",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "attribution": "",
      "description": "Malik Ambar's Tomb, Khuldabad, Aurangabad district, Maharashtra. Malik Ambar (1549–1626 AD), a prominent figure in the political history of the Deccan, served as the minister of the Nizamshahi dynasty of Ahmadnagar and held the position of commander-in-chief of its army. His mausoleum stands on an elevated square platform made of basalt. The structure itself is square in plan, with each façade featuring three arches. The central arch on each side is intricately adorned with jali work, primarily showcasing geometric patterns, while the arches on either side are false. The façade is further embellished with recesses and cusped arches, and the central arch on the southern side serves as the main entrance. Each arch includes stylised flower petals hanging inward, enriched with delicate plaster tracery resembling lacework. The bracketed cornice and overhanging eaves are still well-preserved. Above the cornice, the parapet displays a trefoil design, and just above the arches on each side is a row of miniature false arches. The dome, large and bulbous in form with a hemispherical shape, rises from a constricted base encircled by lotus petal motifs at its lower edge. A tall and elegant finial, topped with a metal crescent, crowns the dome. Small kiosks are placed on all four corners above broad lintels. Inside, the sarcophagus is crafted from polished black stone. The tomb of Malik Ambar—simple yet refined and elegant—stands as a timeless representation of his character and remains an exemplary specimen of Nizamshahi architecture.",
